{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact B head ed669893db96b331055ce0adb521d9f61e4a8d7a on builder/shared-descriptions-t0b-20261110, tree 1151a9d9, all 28 artifacts, for request 260991d8 under promise b1536885 and review request 24b216ef. Client now owns the declaration descriptions and the mark wording. MCP and the CLI browser helpers read them from Client, and `docs/lanes-reference.md` and `docs/mcp-tools.json` stay byte-identical. The head follows rulings f917e28d and 7fb1a2da.\n\nWhat I checked in the source:\n\n- The factory. `descriptionsOf(catalogue)` in `packages/client/src/describe.ts` closes `typeWords`, `fieldLines`, `sentence`, `guardSentences`, `describeAct`, `schemaOf` and `actInputSchema` over the catalogue it receives, with `MARK_TEXTS`, `markText` and `markRefusals` from `markDescriptionsOf`. `MarkCatalogue` is plain data: pin keys, rule keys and optional refusal names. Client imports only Bytes and holds no setter, registration or default native catalogue. The `BY_NAME` wording rows stay in Client and key on definition names, not pins.\n- Own keys. `markText` and `markRefusals` read the catalogue, the rule code and the `BY_NAME` row with `Object.hasOwn`. A `constructor@1` pin gets the literal fallback.\n- MCP. The private `description-context.ts` projects `VERSIONS` to pin and refusal metadata once and binds one catalogue. The MCP barrel does not export it. `describe.ts` re-exports the 20 independent names from Client and destructures the seven bound members, and `marks.ts` destructures its three. That is the ten bound names as members, with no wrapper functions.\n- The CLI. `browser-helpers.ts` forwards Client's `describeActLine` as `describe` and uses `grantAction`. `describeActLine` binds an empty catalogue inside Client and keeps the CLI's compact wording, including \"moves a issue\". The boundary test now asserts that the browser bundle's inputs include Client's `describe.ts` and no MCP module.\n- The cases. The totality case moves intact into Client's owner, bound to an empty catalogue. The catalogue case binds two catalogues side by side, checks each through one nested sentence, and checks the `constructor@1` fallback. The compact-line case was already in `90db5e7d` before ruling f917e28d, so the ruling's one added case is the catalogue case.\n- The seals. On my own extracted copy of the head, outside the builder's worktree, `scripts/security-entry-points.test.mjs` and `scripts/active-source.test.mjs` pass all 24 cases. That run recomputes the helper row and the three new owner rows.\n- The documents. On the same copy, `apps/room/lanes/scripts/reference.mjs --check` reports `docs/lanes-reference.md` current at 139,830 bytes. Neither document changes against main, and the gate's inventory and definitions owners compare both files.\n\nWhat holds:\n\n- Scope. Main c9055184 is an ancestor, the head is pushed, and the builder's worktree is clean at the head. Against main, the head changes exactly the 28 paths that its 28 artifacts cover. The commit-message check passes: 16 checked, 0 flagged.\n- The history. I compared the old branch at 90c7dd9b with the rebuilt branch at 3cf87cb5. Both hold 15 commits in the same order with the same trees, authors, committers, dates and message bodies. Only merge 45265afa's subject changes, to \"Merge landed shared expected revisions into descriptions\". Both heads have tree c278e416. The old branch and its failed first gate stay unchanged.\n- The gate. I read gate.exit at 3cf87cb5, tree c278e416: \"exit 0\". The launcher, driver and analyzer exits read 0 too. The log records Node, Counting, Plain and Native passed. The reconciliation records 5,838 passes and three expected skips, and no Scale case runs. Only the note and seven evidence manifests change between 3cf87cb5 and the head.\n- The browser. GitHub reports Page run 42784374227 attempt 1 as successful, 12 of 12 steps, and Card run 42784378544 attempt 1 as successful, 13 of 13 steps, both at 90c7dd9b. That source has the head's code tree.\n- The evidence. The 15 manifests verify. Their files match their sizes and digests and are read-only, and the credential scan finds no match.\n\nArchitecture: sound. One portable module owns the wording, and each consumer supplies its catalogue as plain data. Client stays below Platform, and the active-source allowlist does not grow.\n\nSecurity: no finding. The change moves description text and adds no grant, route or setting. Descriptions judge nothing, and the scope still runs every rule. Own-key lookups keep an inherited name from supplying wording or refusals.\n\nSimplification: no required change. One note: the compact and full renderers share `actWords` and `grantWords` through a `compact` flag. A later change to either wording must keep both branches in mind.\n\nThe Page asset changes. The planner decides the deployment. No setting changes. This approval authorizes no deployment, publication or live acceptance. DX-T0 6f87a4e3 stays open.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b2236f150c53b1f1dd7d7ca2e22ff9960bc6cc06","binding":"assigned","head":"ed669893db96b331055ce0adb521d9f61e4a8d7a","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:09ace98bd21c15e6c90a8846f27a55b21801dbca\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b153688569e2a6c32e95e32d3c5c0a22c9a01f3c\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:09ace98bd21c15e6c90a8846f27a55b21801dbca","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}